INT. FAMILY STATION WAGON, PARKED OUTSIDE A PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

Rain beads the windshield. The dashboard clock reads 7:52. Across the street, the planetarium dome glows a soft blue, waiting for a show that has already begun.

In the driver's seat, a man in a thin wool coat grips the wheel, his eyes fixed on the building. This is the STRANGER: late forties, heavy-lidded, thoughtful. In the back seat, a girl of ten sits cross-legged, her ticket pinched between two fingers. She watches him in the rearview mirror and says nothing.

The man draws a breath to speak. Doesn't.

The girl unbuckles, leans forward between the seats.

GIRL
We're late.

STRANGER
I know.

GIRL
Mom says you're always late.

STRANGER
(a small laugh)
Your mother is usually right.

GIRL
Is that why she left?

He doesn't answer. A raindrop slides down the glass. The windshield wipers sweep once, twice.

STRANGER
What do you want me to say, Josephine?

JOSEPHINE
(shrugging)
Just say a true thing.

The man looks at her, startled. Something in his shoulders loosens.

STRANGER
All right. I don't know how to do this. I've never known. I walk into rooms and everyone's already decided who I am.

JOSEPHINE
You're my dad.

STRANGER
Yes.

JOSEPHINE
That's all you have to be.

His phone buzzes on the dashboard. A name lights the screen: MAEVE. He lets it ring.

JOSEPHINE (CONT'D)
Aren't you going to get it?

STRANGER
Later.

The phone goes silent, then buzzes again. Josephine reaches over and answers it for him, putting it on speaker.

MAEVE (V.O.)
(over phone, tense)
Desmond? Is she with you? The planetarium said you never checked in.

DESMOND
(clearing his throat)
She's here, Maeve. We're in the car.

MAEVE (V.O.)
(over phone)
In the car? It started at seven.

DESMOND
I know. I got us lost. And then I just... sat here.

A pause on the line. Rain on the roof.

MAEVE (V.O.)
(over phone, softer)
Is she okay?

JOSEPHINE
^I'm okay.

DESMOND
^She's okay.

They say it at the same time. Desmond glances at his daughter. She smiles.

MAEVE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Then go in. They'll let you in late. Tell them it's my fault.

DESMOND
It isn't.

MAEVE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Just go, Des.

The call ends. Desmond sets the phone down very gently, as if it might break.

JOSEPHINE
So?

DESMOND
So.

He opens his door. The rain is barely more than mist. He walks around, opens hers, and offers his hand. She takes it.

DESMOND (CONT'D)
Let's go look at the sky.

They cross the street together, small under the glowing dome.
